Inner functions and outer functions are two types of functions in JavaScript that have different scopes and access to variables.

1. Inner Functions:
Inner functions are functions defined within another function. They have access to the variables and parameters of the outer function, as well as any variables declared within the inner function itself. Inner functions can be used to create closures, which are functions that retain access to their lexical scope even when called later.

Example:
```javascript
function outerFunction() {
  let x = 5;
  let y = 10;

  function innerFunction() {
    console.log(x); // 5
    console.log(y); // 10
    console.log(this); // Window object
  }

  innerFunction();
}

outerFunction();
```
In this example, the `innerFunction` is an inner function defined within the `outerFunction`. It has access to the variables `x` and `y`, as well as the `this` keyword, which refers to the global object (in this case, the `Window` object).

2. Outer Functions:
Outer functions are functions defined outside of any other function. They have access to the variables and parameters passed to them, as well as any variables declared within the outer function itself. Outer functions cannot directly access the variables of inner functions, but they can return values or use callbacks to communicate with inner functions.

Example:
```javascript
function outerFunction(x) {
  let y = 10;

  function innerFunction() {
    console.log(x); // 5
    console.log(y); // 10
    console.log(this); // Window object
  }

  return innerFunction;
}

let innerFunction = outerFunction(5);
innerFunction();
```
In this example, the `outerFunction` is an outer function that returns an inner function. The inner function has access to the variable `x`, which is passed to the `outerFunction`, as well as the `y` variable, which is declared within the `outerFunction`. The `this` keyword refers to the global object (in this case, the `Window` object).

In summary, inner functions have access to the variables and parameters of the outer

