You can use a regular expression to automatically prefix a string before passing it in as a regexp. Here's an example using JavaScript:

```javascript
function autoPrefix(input) {
  return `^${input}$`;
}

const regexp = autoPrefix("myRegexp");
console.log(regexp); // ^myRegexp$
```

In this example, the `autoPrefix` function takes an input string and returns a regular expression that includes the input string surrounded by anchor characters (`^` and `$`) to make it a full regex pattern. The resulting regex pattern can then be used for matching or searching.

