To configure your Chrome extension's boilerplate files to inject JavaScript on an existing webpage, you need to follow these steps:

1. Create a `manifest.json` file in the root directory of your extension. This file will contain information about your extension, such as its name, version, and permissions.
2. In the `manifest.json` file, add the following code to declare the necessary permissions for your extension to inject scripts:
```json
{
  "manifest_version": 2,
  "name": "Your Extension Name",
  "version": "1.0",
  "description": "A Chrome extension that injects JavaScript into webpages.",
  "permissions": ["activeTab", "tabs", "https://*/*", "http://*/*"]
}
```
This code grants your extension permission to access the active tab, tabs, and any HTTP or HTTPS URL.
3. Create a `background.js` file in the root directory of your extension. This file will contain the code that runs in the background when your extension is installed.
4. In the `background.js` file, add the following code to create a content script that will be injected into the active tab:
```javascript
chrome.runtime.onInstalled.addListener(details => {
  if (details.reason === 'install') {
    chrome.tabs.query({active: true, currentWindow: true}, tabs => {
      chrome.tabs.executeScript(tabs[0].id, {file: 'content.js'});
    });
  }
});
```
This code listens for the `onInstalled` event, which is triggered when your extension is installed. When the event is triggered, it queries the active tab and executes the `content.js` script in the background.
5. Create a `content.js` file in the root directory of your extension. This file will contain the code that runs in the content script.
6. In the `content.js` file, add the following code to inject the JavaScript code into the webpage:
```javascript
