</RemoveWidows>
```

To update your application without replacing the executable, you can follow these steps:

1. Create a new folder in the same directory as the existing executable. For example, create a folder named "update".
2. Extract the contents of the ZIP file into the "update" folder.
3. Modify the existing executable to check for updates and install them if available. You can use a library like Updater.NET to help with this task.
4. When an update is available, the executable will download the update files from the "update" folder and install them.
5. After the installation is complete, the executable will restart itself using the updated files.

Here's some sample code to get you started:
```csharp
using System;
using System.IO;
using System.Text;
using UpdaterDotNet;

namespace MyApplication
{
    class Program
    {
        static void Main(string[] args)
        {
            // Check for updates
            var updater = new Updater("update");
            if (updater.IsUpdateAvailable())
            {
                // Download and install the update
                updater.DownloadAndInstallUpdate();
            }

            // Restart the application
            Process.Start(typeof(Program).Assembly.Location);
        }
    }
}
```
This code creates an instance of the `Updater` class and checks if an update is available. If an update is available, it downloads and installs the update, then restarts the application using the updated files.

Note that this is just a basic example, and you may need to modify it to fit your specific needs. Additionally, you should ensure that your application is designed to handle updates smoothly, and that you have tested the update process thoroughly before deploying it to production.

